  <h1>What Is LoD (Limit of Detection)?</h1>

  <p><strong>Direct answer:</strong> LoD (limit of detection) is the lowest analyte concentration a test can reliably distinguish from zero — the smallest amount the assay can actually "see". It is an analytical property of the assay, distinct from the clinical cut-off used to call a result positive.</p>

  <h2>LoD vs cut-off</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>LoD</th><th>Cut-off</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>What it is</th><td>Analytical detection floor</td><td>Clinical decision threshold</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Question it answers</th><td>How little can the test detect?</td><td>What counts as positive?</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Relationship</th><td>Usually below the cut-off</td><td>Usually set above the LoD</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How LoD is determined</h2>
  <p>LoD is established experimentally by measuring replicate blank and low-concentration samples and determining the concentration that yields a signal reliably above background. It is a validation parameter, not a fixed universal number — it varies by assay and matrix.</p>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <p>LoD determines a test's ability to catch low analyte levels — critical for early detection and low-viral-load cases. A lower LoD means higher analytical sensitivity, which is one reason molecular (PCR) tests detect earlier than antigen rapid tests.</p>

  <h2>Related terms</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sensitivity</strong> — the proportion of true positives detected (clinical, not analytical).</li>
    <li><strong>Limit of quantification (LoQ)</strong> — the lowest concentration measured with acceptable precision (quantitative assays).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <div class="card">
    <p><strong>Is LoD the same as cut-off?</strong> No — LoD is the analytical detection floor; cut-off is the clinical threshold for a positive call.</p>
    <p><strong>Why does a lower LoD matter?</strong> It lets the test detect lower analyte levels, improving early detection.</p>
  </div>

